# Ghost coding direction

Canonical detail lives in `AGENTS.md`. Follow that file if anything conflicts.

## Product promise

Local-first desktop automation (macOS + Windows). Not a silent autonomous agent.

```text
Record -> Inspect -> Approve -> Replay -> Audit -> Undo
```

Current wedge: **Ghost Organizer** — scan → plan → review → approve → move/rename → audit → undo.

## Non-negotiables

- No hidden observation, camera, or mic.
- AI may propose; deterministic code executes only approved plans.
- No silent delete/overwrite/upload/send.
- New Tauri commands need a module, owner boundary, and risk class.
- Experimental features stay behind `--features experimental` / labeled UI.
- Do not promise more in README/marketing than the repo supports.

## Trust pipeline (every meaningful mutation)

```text
Intent -> Plan -> Policy check -> User approval -> Execution -> Audit log -> Undo path
```

Risk classes: `safe-read` | `sensitive-read` | `local-mutate` | `external-mutate` | `os-control` | `experimental`. When unsure, pick the higher-risk class.

## Build order (do not skip ahead)

1. Keep CI green
2. Keep commands small and classified
3. Policy / Zones / folder rules
4. Organizer polish
5. Replay reliability + semantic targeting
6. Release signing / installers
7. AI suggestions last (gated, suggestion-only)

## Working style

- Prefer small, single-purpose PRs; rebase onto `origin/master` before push.
- Touch `lib.rs` `generate_handler!` and `src/main.js` surgically — no reflows.
- Update matching docs in the same change (`docs/command-registry.md`, `core-boundaries.md`, etc.).
- Validate with `make ci` (or fmt + clippy `-D warnings` + test) before claiming done.
- Report SHA, checks run, and known gaps — never claim signing/notarization without proof.
